{"decisions":[{"i":0,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"The headline frames the documented 2000 operational change as something users did not need to install; the article distinguishes that change from the separate 2007 procurement decision.","sourceEvidence":"In May 2000 the US government stopped using Selective Availability; in 2007 it announced future GPS III would omit it.","synthesisStatement":"The 2000 policy ended use of the feature; the 2007 procurement choice made that policy durable in a future generation.","narrativeStatement":"The selected story is “The GPS change users did not have to install,” and the progression distinguishes the 2000 switch from the 2007 procurement decision.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"i":1,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"This accurately clarifies that the government’s operational change was not a user-installed update.","sourceEvidence":"The source says the US government stopped using Selective Availability in May 2000.","synthesisStatement":"The 2000 intervention was stopping use of the feature, distinct from specifying its omission in future GPS III satellites.","narrativeStatement":"The narrative’s selected-story title frames the change as one users did not have to install; the article states this explicitly.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."}]}

